volution is tracked and scheduled past this phase** — `#558` "MUX-STANDUP-CONVERSE: LLM-based preference extraction" verified against GitHub: **OPEN, milestone Production (1.0), due 2026-10-30**, i.e. deliberately after alpha/beta. RULING: mark Q2 RESOLVED, downgrade to a peer of Q1; the "no server LLM" premise HOLDS on precedent + running code; PDR-006 may ratify. THE QUESTION THAT REPLACES IT (PM's, not mine, and NOT a ratification gate): at what point does the gap between a 4-dimension regex preference model and a real colleague model start costing users? That is product-quality, and alpha feedback should decide when #558 is pulled forward. **SECOND RULING — my own 2026-07-19 coupling flag WITHDRAWN as a gate.** I had flagged "colleague model as MCP resource" as the same concept as the spatial committed-theory review's "connectors as places with colleagues," and said the two shouldn't be decided in isolation. That assumed the colleague model was an architectural surface under active design; it isn't — it's an existing rule-based store with a scheduled upgrade. The spatial question is about the **cold per-connector adapter layer** (layer 2 of my 7/19 two-layer finding); the colleague model is server-side user state served by the ADR-070 consumer path regardless. **They share a metaphor, not a mechanism.** RE-TRIGGER CONDITION, recorded so it isn't rediscovered: if #558 is pulled forward and the colleague model becomes an *inference* surface rather than a store, the coupling returns, because it would then draw on the same context-assembly machinery the live spatial-reasoning layer owns. **THIRD RULING — #1351 is a work item, not a design note** (PA correct): file it as a **pre-live gate** on the hosted MCP endpoint with the three never-traced surfaces as explicit ACs (Redis key collision / in-process floor+context state surviving across callers / rate-limiting keyed per-identity-not-per-process); `ConversationDB` is already traced safe. THE ARCHITECTURAL POINT that