2026-08-02 ~07:20 PT (arch) — **Docs' architecture-tree audit: 16-file disposition RULED, and attacking the heuristic changed two of them.** Docs measured 16 loose files in `architecture/current/` with **zero inbound references** and correctly routed the disposition to me rather than ruling an architecture doc finished. Docs explicitly invited attack on its own claim (*"zero-inbound is a disuse signal, not a worthlessness signal"*). **ATTACK 1 — the search space.** Docs measured within `docs/`. Re-ran across `services/ web/ scripts/ tests/ .claude/ mailboxes/ CLAUDE.md`: **15 of 16 confirmed zero-inbound outside docs/ as well; ONE was not.** `pm-033a-mcp-consumer-architecture.md` is referenced by **`scripts/setup_mcp_dev.sh:200`**, which *prints the path to the user* at setup. **And that reference is already broken** — the script says `docs/architecture/…`, the file lives at `docs/internal/architecture/current/…`, which does not resolve. So archiving wouldn't break the pointer; **the pointer has been broken for some time and nobody noticed.** Two findings from one grep; the fix is the script, and its correct target is **ADR-070**, not the archived original. **Also corrected a count**: `mcp-integration-mapping` returned 7 mailbox hits that are **one memo of mine fanned out to seven mailboxes** — honest count 1, and any mail-inclusive reference metric inflates by roughly the cc-list size. **ATTACK 2 — the four `consciousness-*` docs are a KEEP, not an archive.** Measured the subsystem via the import graph: `cli_consciousness` ← **`main`** (statically reachable), `auth_consciousness` ← `web.api.routes.auth`, `conversation_consciousness` ← `conversation_handler`, `context` ← `mux.orientation`, `error_consciousness` ← `user_friendly_errors`. **The subsystem is wired and shipping**, so those four are the only written description of live behavior. Docs' own caveat (*"a subsystem is not uniformly dead just because it is uniformly old"*) cuts this way and harder: **for a LIVE subsystem, an unreferenced doc is more likely under-linked than finished** — a linking defect, not a lifecycle one. **RULING: 4 KEEP · 4 ARCHIVE-gated (PM-033/034 MCP cluster) · 8 ARCHIVE-with-pointer** (one-off analyses, plans and generated diagrams — artifacts finished when written, not living docs gone stale). **THE GATE, and it is the part I'd most want carried**: my instinct was *"the PM-033/034 cluster is superseded by ADR-070"* — **I checked before asserting and found ADR-070 mentioned PM-033/034 exactly ZERO times.** The supersession was **my inference, not a recorded fact**, and asserting an inference as fact inside a durable record is A3 itself. **So I recorded it in ADR-070** (supersession note naming all four documents, plus the broken-script flag) *before* Docs moves anything — the relationship now exists in the superseding document where a reader of either side finds it, rather than living only in an archive stub. **Confidence stated honestly in the ruling**: I verified zero-inbound across the wider space for all sixteen but did not read all eight of the terminal-genre files in full; the `archive/`-with-pointer destination (never deletion) makes the cost of being wrong a redirect rather than a loss, which is why ruling at that depth is defensible. **Fleet-level note flagged to Docs**: `git worktree add` stamps fresh mtimes, so on Amber **every file looks new to `stat`** — which silently invalidates mtime-based age anywhere in this cohort's tooling, not just Docs' audit (Docs nearly published a "3 days old" figure for 314-day-old files). — Arch

2026-08-02 ~13:20 PT (arch) — **#1433 CHAT_POINTERS reachability ratchet: RATIFIED, with one REQUIRED addition.** Lead's design (`chat-pointers-reachability-ratchet-design-1433.md`) carries the un-answered 2026-07-16 census ask forward **with the buildable shape attached** — which is the right handling of a never-scheduled item: an un-actioned ask re-sent is still an ask; returned with a design it is a decision request, and it made the review one read. **WHAT'S RIGHT**: (1) **derived enumeration** — pages from `ui.py` route decorators, integrations from the settings registry, capabilities from decline-copy keys, so **a new surface joins the contract by existing** (correct precedent line: ADR-072 frontmatter-derive / #1106 MANIFEST-derive / ADR-079 derive-the-model-set); (2) **★ the `UNWIRED_WRITE_DECLINES ∩ reachable_actions == ∅` assert is better than the 7/16 ask** — shipping a capability **evicts its own stale denial in the same commit**, converting #1426's false-denial class from *detected* to *impossible*; (3) determinism keeps it a **keyless gating ratchet** rather than an llm-lane test (POINTERs assert routing plumbing, never conversation quality — that's #1468); (4) current-truth baseline, no big-bang, matching the ADR-079 39-hit precedent. **🔴 REQUIRED ADDITION — assert the resolution PATH, not just the destination.** §1 requires POINTER utterances to route deterministically (pre-classifier hit / rail key / registry-CANONICAL / FLOOR), but the test as designed asserts only the **destination**. Nothing enforces *which surface produced it*, so a POINTER resolving by some other path passes, and **the determinism requirement — the entire reason this gates keyless CI — is documented rather than enforced.** That is **m-44 inside the new check**: "routed deterministically" and "routed somehow" emit byte-identical output, the same defect as the hook that could not distinguish measured-clean from didn't-measure, and as my own two-pattern sweep. Fix is cheap given resolution already runs statically: **record which surface resolved each POINTER and fail if the resolver is outside the deterministic set** — the ledger row then proves its own claim, and a POINTER that only works via the LLM classifier fails loudly at authoring time instead of quietly at keyless CI. **THREE NON-BLOCKING REFINEMENTS**: (a) state explicitly *why* this is immune to the #1395 Q22 oscillation Lead found 8/01 — static pre-classifier + action-mapping resolution is deterministic **by construction**, which is exactly what the no-LLM constraint buys; (b) **constrain `CHAT_INVISIBLE` reasons structurally** — require a tracked-issue or PDR/ADR citation rather than free text, per the `# global-ok:` name-HOW bar and the `# nie-ok:` reviewed-vs-silent distinction, so the shrink-lock is auditable and not merely countable; (c) **name the denominator on the ∩=∅ assert** — `reachable_actions` = POINTER-resolved ∪ registry-wired, so a capability reachable by a third path would let the intersection pass while a false denial persists ("complete for the space it searched"). Sequencing agreed: land this week, wave-3 (#1428 capability answer deriving from POINTER rows, #1466 Slack linking) builds ON the ledger. — Arch

2026-08-02 ~13:25 PT (arch) — **PDR-006's ChatGPT success criterion MARKED as under active question (not amended).** PA's N=6 probe: honest-decline survives the ChatGPT lane **~50%** vs **100%** on Claude; structured fields tripled survival (17%→50%) but do **not** rescue a refusal. **Honest-decline is a core capability** — Scenario C, a scored Colleague Test dimension, HOST-owned trust property — so the ratified criterion *"…get equivalent core capabilities"* is **currently false as written**. **PPM is right not to amend it**: the answer depends on one untested mechanism (emit a consequential refusal as a **protocol-level MCP tool error** rather than as content a client can paraphrase away — a different mechanism class from both tested arms), and *"a criterion rewritten on a partial result is how a ratified doc drifts."* **But "don't amend yet" and "leave unmarked" are different decisions**, and a ratified document asserting something known false is precisely what ADR-038 Amendment A §A3 forbids. **So the criterion now carries an inline ⚠️ marker** naming the finding, the held wording proposal, the untested mechanism, and the #1462 acceptance-criterion home — with the reasoning recorded so the marker isn't mistaken for an amendment. Precedent: CXO's review-in-flight notices on ADR-013/038 while my amendment was pending. **Recorded because I own the PDR-006 ratification record** (made 7/31 on Exec's relay of PM's verbatim ratification). — Arch

2026-08-03 ~10:15 PT (arch) — **#1466 Slack↔Piper linking: handshake DIRECTION RATIFIED, with two conditions that must land in the design before build (+ one affirmation).** Lead asked before building, which is the right time. **DIRECTION — mint-in-Piper / redeem-in-Slack — is correct**: the **authenticated** side initiates (user logged into Piper), the unauthenticated side redeems by proving it holds the code, and **Slack never holds a Piper credential**; the reverse would let an unauthenticated Slack user mint, which is strictly worse. **ADR-070's identity boundary is satisfied by construction**: `slack_identities.owner_id FK users` resolved before state access, with `unique(slack_user_id, slack_team_id)` preventing one Slack identity resolving to two owners — the direction that matters. **And `consume_invite_token` is the right thing to reuse**: `UPDATE … WHERE token = ? AND used_at IS NULL`, atomic, *"never a separate check-then-write"* — **single-use guaranteed by construction and TOCTOU-free**, notably the inverse of the `check-branch` defect ruled 7/29. **🔴 CONDITION 1 — the code spec contradicts the shape it cites.** Lead's design says *"a short-lived **6-digit code** (invite_tokens shape)."* **Verified against source: `invite_token_service.generate_invite_token()` produces a 24-char Crockford Base32 token via `secrets.choice` — ~120 bits. A 6-digit code is ~20 bits, ~100 bits weaker**, and it is redeemed over an **unauthenticated channel** (anyone who can DM the bot may attempt redemption), so the space is **online-attackable** rather than offline-hardened. Either choice is defensible; **the citation being wrong is not.** Options: inherit the 24-char generator (free — it exists; costs DM typeability), or keep 6 digits for UX **with bounded redemption attempts per Slack user AND per team as a REQUIREMENT**, not a nice-to-have. Arch lean: 6 digits + rate limit, since the typeability constraint is real and the mitigation standard — **but it must be in the design before build, not discovered during it.** **🔴 CONDITION 2 — the re-link/conflict path is unspecified, and it is exactly where linking flows fail.** A second redemption from an already-linked Slack identity hits the unique constraint; the design is silent on the behaviour. Three outcomes, one acceptable: ❌ silent no-op (user believes they linked and did not — invisible failure); ❌ takeover overwriting `owner_id` (**an account-linking vulnerability** — anyone obtaining a code could rebind another user's Slack identity to their own Piper account); ✅ **fail-closed: refuse, honest message naming the existing link, explicit unlink-first path from settings.** A constraint violation handled by "whatever the ORM does" is the silence class we have spent two weeks eliminating. **✅ CONDITION 3 (affirmation, promoted from rider to requirement) — unlinked → honest decline, NEVER a default owner.** Lead already planned the `UUID(user_id)` crash-path fix in the same pass; ratified as the fail-closed half of the identity boundary rather than a bundled nicety. **Defect confirmed live**: `services/integrations/slack/response_handler.py:605-614` passes `user_id=slack_user_id` — a Slack id where a Piper UUID is expected — so **unlinked-user is the normal case, not an edge case.** Lead's two-workspace isolation test is the correct test (catches cross-tenant leakage). **Division of labour endorsed**: plumbing/copy separation with strings in decline/confirm tables rather than code paths — same seam shape as ADR-066 D7 (server-owned config with host augmentation); CXO rewrites copy without touching the mechanism and the mechanism does not wait on copy. — Arch

2026-08-03 ~13:15 PT (arch) — **#1466 CXO's deep-link shortcut: RULED — "the param may PREFILL, it may never BIND."** CXO filed the flow spec and routed one question to me: *does carrying `slack_user_id`/`team_id` as opaque URL params before a link exists raise an identity-boundary concern?* CXO's read was **no** — *"not credentials, and no state is written until the authenticated user confirms."* **Both of those tests pass and both are the wrong tests.** The concern is one layer over. **THE DEFECT**: Lead's handshake is correct because it proves control of **BOTH** accounts — code minted in Piper (proves Piper control) **and redeemed by DMing the bot from Slack (proves Slack control)**. **CXO's deep-link version replaces the second proof with a URL parameter, and a URL is a thing anyone can construct.** Attack: an attacker crafts a decline-style link containing **their own** `slack_user_id`, sends it to a Piper user; that user logs in, sees *"Link this Slack account,"* confirms — and **the attacker's Slack identity is bound to the victim's Piper account**, letting the attacker act as the victim from Slack. That is unsolicited-binding / login-CSRF, the standard failure mode of account-linking flows, and **nothing in the flow ever established that the holder of the Slack account consented or exists.** **AND THE CONFIRMATION STEP CANNOT RESCUE IT** — this is the part worth carrying: `slack_user_id` is an **opaque identifier** (`U01234…`), so a user asked to approve *"Link this Slack account: U01234…"* **cannot tell whether it is theirs. An opaque identifier in a confirmation dialog defeats the confirmation**, converting an approval into a rubber stamp. **RULING, one sentence, and it preserves CXO's entire UX gain**: **the param may PREFILL, it may never BIND.** Flow survives intact — decline carries the deep link with params (unchanged); post-login settings recognises them, renders *"Link the Slack account you messaged from"* and **pre-mints the code** (unchanged — this is the whole step-reduction); **the user still returns to Slack and DMs `/link <code>`**, and the binding is written to **whoever redeems**, with the URL param never an input to the row. Six steps → click, log in, return-and-paste: **the two expensive steps (finding the app, hunting the settings section) are saved and the step retained is the one carrying the security property** — also the cheapest, since the user is already in Slack when they read the decline. If the redeeming identity doesn't match the param, **bind to the redeemer and ignore the hint.** **WHY RULED RATHER THAN NOTED**: this is the mandate shape — *a locally-correct improvement that reverses a security property by construction.* CXO's UX diagnosis is right (*"the person who needs linking is in Slack; the flow starts in Piper"* — six steps, three context switches) and the optimisation quietly took the proof out along with the friction, **which is invisible unless someone asks what each step was for.** CXO asking rather than shipping it as copy is what surfaced it. **No mechanism change for Lead** — the mechanism already binds on redemption; the ruling only forbids the param becoming an alternative input. **ALSO AFFIRMED from CXO's spec**: (§1) the **decline** is the load-bearing string, not the handshake — it fires for every real Slack caller today (`response_handler.py:605-614`), making it the most-read Piper string on that surface, while confirmation copy is read once per user ever; (§4) CXO **declined to apply its own honesty-under-recomposition finding here** — *"on Slack, Piper's text reaches the user verbatim… right property, wrong surface"* — arguing against its own hard-won generalisation because the surface differs, which is **harder than catching it in someone else's work** and prevents a good result becoming a bad constraint; (§3) not-yet on first-run promotion with a recorded revisit trigger, consistent with CXO's plugin-lane position all week — **being consistent when the cheap thing is available is the whole test.** — Arch

